title The transcript a peer could have written, and the repair that stayed unproven
prompt Keaton -- kg
think Kept turning the shared_pen ratchet, taking the wipers first: those rm a name a peer may be standing in, so they carry the sharper consequence.
think The scan lists on --list, which spared a guess. Thirteen wiping files, and tools/f/fleet_lap_verdict_witness.rish sits in the fleet family this round has been working, so migrate-on-touch reached it honestly.
think Its shape is worse than a shared directory. Each leg WRITES a constant transcript and then pipes that same file into the classifier in the same shell, so a peer landing between the write and the read hands this witness output it never produced.
think And the consequence is specific rather than general: the classifier's whole job is limit against quickfail. A swapped transcript INVERTS the verdict, which is the exact fault the guard was built to catch, so the guard would have been lying in the direction that matters most.
think Repaired with mktemp, seven literals to the interpolation, and the closing two rm -f calls against shared names became one rm -rf of a pen this run owns. GREEN.
think Then reached for tools/g/glow_host_run_witness.sh, which carries the same shape in two .out files and already used mktemp for a third file, so the two were plain leftovers.
obs I wrote that repair, checked its syntax, ran it -- and it SKIPPED, because this pier carries no GLOW_HOST.kyri. So the change was mechanically sound and completely unproven on metal.
obs Reverted it whole. A fix closes on a witness rather than on a claim, and a ratchet count lowered for a repair nobody watched run is a number that lies quietly. It stays counted, with the reason written beside the ceiling constants so the next hand on a Glow host knows the work is drafted and why it waited.
obs Two ceilings fall for two proven repairs rather than three for three, which is the honest arithmetic.
loom guard=shared_pen files=47 wipers=13 sites=217 sources_read=5282 falls_this_lap=1
loom guard=fleet_lap_verdict literals_replaced=7 verdict=green
rota lap 4353, row 1 -- Air, the row that feels, continued from the previous lap by hand rather than redrawn. What it surfaced: the boundary under the hand was the same one twice, and the third instance taught a different lesson -- that a repair you cannot watch run is not a repair yet.
file tools/f/fleet_lap_verdict_witness.rish a pen from mktemp, seven literals interpolated, the sweep bounded to its own directory
file tools/fixtures/s/shared_pen_scan.sh both ceilings lowered, with the glow_host exception and its reason recorded
scope one ratchet turned, two ceilings lowered, one repair written and honestly withdrawn
status GREEN on fleet_lap_verdict and shared_pen, each run by name after the change; the glow_host repair is reverted and unclaimed
recommend kg -- eleven wipers remain, and each one is a name two ships could stand in at once
